<section class="block">
<h2>What changed</h2>
<p>OpenAI published an updated Model Spec on 29 October 2026<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. It is reorganised around five values: faithfulness to human intent, harm minimisation, honesty and transparency, human agency, and care<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Most detailed guidance on writing style was retired, with style expected to follow user preferences and context<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. The Spec includes an updated chain of command describing which instructions apply and how conflicts are resolved, with sections marked at different levels of authority<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>, and a new section on how memories and earlier conversations should inform the model's reading of what people want<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p>Two caveats matter. OpenAI describes the Spec as a target, and says its models do not yet fully reflect it<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. And secondary coverage differs on some details of how developer and user instructions are ranked<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>, so read the primary text for anything you depend on.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Why it matters if you build on these models</h2>
<p>A model's published behaviour rules are a moving part of your system. When they change, three things can change with them: how the model resolves conflicts between your system prompt and user requests, how it treats context such as memory and retrieved documents, and how it styles its output. None of that shows up as an API change, so it will not break your build. It can still break your product.</p>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Vendor layer</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Model Spec<em>intended behaviour</em></span><span>Model training<em>moves towards the Spec over time</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Your instructions</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">System prompt<em>scope, priorities, refusals</em></span><span>Tool definitions<em>what the model may do</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Your controls</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Policy layer<em>checks before and after the model</em></span><span>Approval steps<em>for consequential actions</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Your evidence</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Evaluation set<em>re-run on every model change</em></span><span>Telemetry<em>model version on every call</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>The vendor's Spec shapes the model; your prompts, controls and evaluations decide what your product actually does.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Five things to do</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Treat the Spec as context, not a control.</strong> A vendor's behaviour document describes intent. It does not replace your own guardrails for data access, consequential actions or regulated decisions. See <a href="/insights/new-york-youth-chatbot-law-ai-assistants/">policy layers for AI assistants</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Write system prompts that state scope and priorities explicitly.</strong> Do not rely on the model to infer that your instructions outrank user requests. Say what the assistant is for, what it must refuse, and what to do when a user asks for something outside scope.</li>
<li><strong>Keep untrusted content in its place.</strong> Retrieved documents, tool results and emails should be clearly marked as data, and actions that matter should go through checks outside the model. See <a href="/insights/prompt-injection-defence-for-agents/">prompt-injection defence for agents</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Specify style instead of relying on defaults.</strong> With detailed style rules retired from the Spec<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>, house style should live in your prompts and be checked by your evaluations, especially for customer-facing text.</li>
<li><strong>Re-run your evaluation set when the model or Spec changes.</strong> Behaviour will drift as models are trained towards the new Spec<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. Pin model versions where you can, and compare results before upgrading. See <a href="/insights/llm-evaluation-sets-that-predict-production/">evaluation sets that predict production</a>.</li>
</ol>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Know which model answered</h2>
<p>When behaviour changes, the first question is always which model, at which version, produced the output. The OpenTelemetry generative AI conventions record the requested model and the model version that actually responded on every call, alongside token usage<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>. With that in your traces, a shift in behaviour can be tied to a model update rather than guessed at.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/openai-model-spec-update-what-builders-should-do/model-version-trace.webp" alt="Trace detail for a chat call showing span attributes including gen_ai.operation.name, gen_ai.request.model, gen_ai.response.model with a dated model version, finish reasons and input and output token counts" width="1400" height="712" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Every call records the requested model and the exact version that answered, the evidence you need when behaviour changes. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2024/otel-generative-ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenTelemetry blog, Drew Robbins and Liudmila Molkova</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<div class="callout"><p>Vendor behaviour is a dependency like any other. Version it, test it and trace it, or you will learn about changes from your users.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>A routine for every model change</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Watch</b><span>Track model, Spec and policy updates from each vendor you use</span></li>
<li><b>Pin</b><span>Keep production on a known model version until the new one passes</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Evaluate</b><span>Run your evaluation set, including refusals, scope and style cases</span></li>
<li><b>Compare</b><span>Review differences by task, not by headline score</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Adjust</b><span>Update prompts and guardrails where behaviour moved</span></li>
<li><b>Roll out</b><span>Shift traffic gradually and watch telemetry by model version</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>The same routine works for any vendor, not just OpenAI.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>CTO take</h2>
<p>Publishing a detailed Model Spec is useful transparency, and the explicit chain of command will help teams reason about conflicts between their instructions and users'. But the Spec is a statement of intent about a moving target. Teams that ship reliable AI products own their behaviour: explicit prompts, independent controls, evaluations that run on every change and telemetry that names the model behind every answer. Do that, and a vendor's Spec update becomes a routine regression run rather than a production surprise. For the wider week, see the <a href="/insights/tech-news-2026-10-31/">31 October tech news brief</a>.</p>
</section>

